  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe pesky upper level low that has impacted our area the past\nfew days remains over the region this afternoon. This low\ncombined with diurnally-driven instability will result in\nisolated to scattered rain showers this afternoon and possibly\nan isolated thunderstorm. With cool air present aloft (850mb\ntemperatures around +7-8C), small hail will be possible in any \nthunderstorm, or even just a heavier rain shower.\n\nLake effect rain showers are expected to become more \nconcentrated along and north of the NY Thruway corridor tonight, \nbefore a shortwave moving through results in the flow turning\nmore northwesterly on Tuesday. This will allow for isolated to\nscattered lake effect/lake enhanced rain showers to spread \nfarther south and east once again through Central NY and \npotentially into Northeast PA. PoPs were increased from the NBM \nboth this afternoon and Tuesday afternoon as the NBM tends to be\nunderdone with shower coverage and not far enough southeast in \nthis setup.\n\nTemperature-wise, it will feel a bit fall-like both today and\nTuesday with highs only in the 60s and lower 70s, potentially\nmid 70s in some of the valleys on Tuesday. Overnight lows\ntonight will also be on the cooler side, with lows in the upper\n40s to mid 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure briefly builds in Tuesday night into Wednesday \nwith dry conditions. The next cold front starts to approach \nfrom the west Thursday and moves through Thursday night into \nearly Friday with scattered showers and thunderstorms. An area \nof low pressure may form near the Southeast Coast and lift north\nahead of the front and enhance the moisture across the area \nwhich could result in some localized downpours. High pressure \nlooks to build back in with drier conditions for the start of \nnext weekend. \n\nTemperatures will be on the rise Wednesday through Friday with \nhighs generally in the mid 70s to near 80 degrees. The Lake \nPlain and portions of the Finger Lakes do look a bit warmer on \nThursday ahead of the front with some places reaching the lower \nto mid 80s.",
